#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21691c25-85fe-50d4-8992-94793dce0cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275780} {:answer/id #uuid "25bc0f7d-35fe-5f7e-8ee1-1db7cf2b62c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275819} {:answer/id #uuid "52b4bc38-7d7f-5677-a106-02e57bec4368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276211} {:answer/id #uuid "8d37e41f-2bdb-5a1a-b86f-773de0e20f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276756} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc124b6-2d46-5e4f-a491-7d52913d1bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d37e41f-2bdb-5a1a-b86f-773de0e20f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276756} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ee44d9-0fea-51a9-b385-74b8a13e1ce7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35b15061-fc8b-55f3-8742-4ca105208a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-161624" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\nII. $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\nIII. $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/84/8414de2e05da43c03fae3c8b1ff2102401e990188e9fd4ee59a24308ec34b086.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. As we have seen, $∂A$ consists of two concentric circles: the outer one is in $A,$ while the inner one is not.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains some of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57716a2f-adbe-53dc-9a36-7e949c3676fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276258} {:answer/id #uuid "72118cf0-8b8a-5295-919c-27f5cbbdd835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276510} {:answer/id #uuid "87dc8626-1873-5666-be98-d2e39efb8926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276704} {:answer/id #uuid "a38f3455-f66a-5215-a435-be8ebbaf60e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276950} {:answer/id #uuid "bc63d771-387a-52c7-99fd-876c0770a2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57716a2f-adbe-53dc-9a36-7e949c3676fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276258} :answer-field/id #uuid "89bbeb67-b26e-5d94-986d-584288de76a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32d0249e-396e-5abf-9c3e-a9a01870f500" :question/math-academy-id "q-161643" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4y>{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n\nII. $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n\nIII. $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a380742f47d97062d56b482592cd2d2961dcc40262131a99886b815c507ab6e3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26d264d702368ed6fe05577d7c34630bd16b41bdba8d206dd4db39763db02938.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊈A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192786 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052b75ab-358d-5073-b438-8aabbcf16598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278638} {:answer/id #uuid "21f1b1b1-4cbf-5384-abf1-011fb4b8e732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278897} {:answer/id #uuid "62d28ecd-0906-545c-93e9-7da6ccef73dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279488} {:answer/id #uuid "84b65459-6cd3-563c-a465-2e40b3ffdf57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279785} {:answer/id #uuid "97292980-0d47-5775-9ecb-d399051901ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97292980-0d47-5775-9ecb-d399051901ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279929} :answer-field/id #uuid "f48d194b-9d36-54e9-be97-ae912aef2d30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46773dfb-2950-5619-a7ba-c3559b8d83bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161652" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. $∂A\\cap A\\ne ∅$\n\nII. $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n\nIII. $A=\\text{int}\\,A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/22/2273cd843c4bfaeaff10d49d3400d7661262673639a66034638d805eef5b1e47.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/5e/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have  $∂A\\cap A=∅.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III  only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ed68dd-7aef-5656-ac3a-0cafe1572795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x<2,\\text{ }0\\le y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278794} {:answer/id #uuid "38d4ea31-5c92-59e1-b1b8-86206c057d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\text{ }0<y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279088} {:answer/id #uuid "5124a441-ffdf-5fb7-9c90-48ab336f851d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<2,\\text{ }0\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279331} {:answer/id #uuid "df496dda-7913-50b6-b84c-a2e72d9ca9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<2,\\text{ }0<y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280560} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4012d7-37e1-5799-babd-f5c1ef441092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 2,\\text{ }0\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df496dda-7913-50b6-b84c-a2e72d9ca9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<2,\\text{ }0<y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280560} :answer-field/id #uuid "74aee92b-a504-5e91-8eb4-d4d52bb0c27d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb88fa68-158a-5357-aaca-59045c1933c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161721" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6debac35386e085a0197cf9eb3f2da65cc719ace67163cad625f58c056fb8f67.png)\n\nConsider the set $A$ shown above. The interior of $A$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA}}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/c0/c0b805c707981b9b6fcfa2ed387ad32a024fc845e6fd355a4b751ede4ad6a97e.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/69/69708aa698c38f8a29d3eb69083f893fd8fca183bd713387456624fc38b86fe9.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4,\\,0<x<2,\\text{ }0<y<2\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f89386f9-8a2b-56eb-8521-7c1ddafcfd96" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Interior of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afb66ed2-895b-5f65-920c-57e9c4e712a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<y<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379786} {:answer/id #uuid "c89a07de-de11-5729-9c3f-59a3dce504fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379787} {:answer/id #uuid "27aebc95-7034-501b-b146-69c5c61f0fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379788} {:answer/id #uuid "f05d76fa-181c-5c72-bf35-0a16895ff797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<y<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379789} {:answer/id #uuid "bca2f207-84e6-5811-b1b2-ec4c412cc4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bca2f207-84e6-5811-b1b2-ec4c412cc4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<y<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379790} :answer-field/id #uuid "0884985a-f4ec-5536-b90e-64a7f3d92f99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13c6da4f-5008-5951-9c9e-dc34e9db3f48" :question/math-academy-id "q-35879" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,-1\\le y<0\\}.\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}\\,\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc71859cb4d1e58ea3d15c2da66bce014898281fe237f2e0c2396f96c84c9e79.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ef/eff037b96b6a42120fdfa112a98d1e0749cdd56bd89acd647db448b08d7323ad.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<x<2,\\,-1<y<0\\,\\}.\n$$"}]}
